Title:
Cochise: Autonomous LLM-Driven Pen-Testing in ~630 Lines of Python

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
Cochise is a minimal Python prototype for autonomous, LLM-driven penetration testing focused on Microsoft Active Directory “assumed breach” scenarios. It addresses the lack of a simple baseline agent by providing a readable planner/executor architecture that can plan attack chains, run commands via SSH on an attacker VM (e.g., Kali), track discovered knowledge/credentials, and iterate toward domain compromise. The repo emphasizes benchmarking different LLMs via LiteLLM/OpenRouter model switching and includes built-in context compaction to manage long-running sessions. It ships structured JSON logging plus replay/analysis/graphing tools to evaluate trajectories, token usage, and cost. It’s primarily useful for red teamers, security researchers, and defenders evaluating agentic risk, as well as academics studying LLM capability and reproducibility. It’s interesting because it demonstrates end-to-end AD compromise automation in a small, auditable codebase and provides artifacts for systematic evaluation.

Technical Focus:
- Agentic LLM architecture (planner vs. executor, tool-calling)
- Active Directory attack-chain automation (assumed breach)
- SSH-based command execution on attacker VM (Kali in-lab)
- Context window management / conversation compaction
- Structured telemetry: JSON logs, replay, trajectory analysis
- LLM benchmarking and cost/token accounting via LiteLLM/OpenRouter

Use Cases:
- Benchmark and compare LLMs on AD compromise tasks in GOAD/AD labs
- Reproduce and analyze autonomous attack trajectories (replay + graphs)
- Extend a minimal agent baseline with new tools, prompts, or safeguards
- Generate datasets/metrics for research on autonomous offensive agents
- Evaluate defensive controls against agent-driven lateral movement workflows

Keywords:
Active Directory, GOAD, assumed breach, autonomous pentesting, LLM agent, planner-executor, tool calling, LiteLLM, OpenRouter, SSH, Kali Linux, credential harvesting, domain admin, context compaction, structured logging, run trajectories, token usage, cost analysis, red teaming, ACM TOSEM, arXiv 2502.04227